@page "/events/tutorials/02-state"

<TutorialDetail Tutorials="TutorialSet.Events" Slug="02-state">
<p><strong>Goal:</strong> share a value between components anywhere in the tree, so it updates everywhere when it changes, and keep re-renders to the components that actually use the changed part.</p>

<p>State is the hub's version of a Svelte store: it <strong>always has a current value</strong>, so a component that appears later still sees it. If you only need to say "something happened", use an <a href="/events/tutorials/04-events">event</a> instead.</p>

<p><em>See it running:</em> the <strong>Shared state</strong> sample page (<a href="/events/example/shared-counter"><code>/events/example/shared-counter</code></a>).</p>

<h2 id="1-define-the-state">1. Define the state</h2>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/CounterState.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Counter;

public sealed record CounterState(int Count, int Step) : IState&lt;CounterState&gt;
{
    public static CounterState Initial =&gt; new(Count: 0, Step: 1);
}</code></pre>

<p>Three rules:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Implement <code>IState&lt;TSelf&gt;</code></strong> and provide <code>static Initial</code>. The compiler requires it. It's the value the store starts with, so no DI registration is needed.</li>
<li><strong>Use a <code>record</code>.</strong> The store compares old and new values with record equality and skips notifications when nothing changed. A <code>class</code> would notify on every write (analyzer <code>RFEVT013</code> warns about this).</li>
<li><strong>Keep it immutable.</strong> You change state by replacing the value, never by mutating it.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="2-write-to-it">2. Write to it</h2>

<p>Any <code>RfEventComponent</code> can write through <code>State&lt;T&gt;()</code>, which returns the store:</p>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/CounterButtons.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;div class="btn-group"&gt;
    &lt;button class="btn btn-primary" @@onclick="Increment"&gt;+ step&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button class="btn btn-outline-secondary" @@onclick="Reset"&gt;Reset&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/CounterButtons.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Counter.Components;

public partial class CounterButtons
{
    // Update = atomic read-modify-write; safe even if several threads write at once (Blazor Server).
    private void Increment() =&gt; State&lt;CounterState&gt;().Update(s =&gt; s with { Count = s.Count + s.Step });

    // Set = replace the whole value.
    private void Reset() =&gt; State&lt;CounterState&gt;().Set(CounterState.Initial);
}</code></pre>

<p><code>CounterButtons</code> writes but doesn't read the state in its markup, so it doesn't re-render when the count changes. That's what you want for a component that only writes.</p>

<h2 id="3-read-it-reactively">3. Read it reactively</h2>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/CounterDisplay.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;p class="display-6"&gt;Count: @@Counter.Count (step @@Counter.Step)&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/CounterDisplay.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Counter.Components;

public partial class CounterDisplay
{
    [UseState]
    private partial CounterState Counter { get; }
}</code></pre>

<p><code>[UseState]</code> on a <code>partial</code> get-only property makes the property <strong>always return the current value</strong> and <strong>re-renders the component whenever the value changes</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="4-re-render-less-with-a-selector">4. Re-render less with a selector</h2>

<p>A component that only uses part of the state shouldn't re-render when another part changes. Give <code>[UseState]</code> a selector: a static method that picks out what the component needs. The component re-renders only when the selected value changes.</p>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/StepPicker.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;label&gt;
    Step
    &lt;select class="form-select" value="@@Step" @@onchange="Change"&gt;
        &lt;option value="1"&gt;1&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="5"&gt;5&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;option value="10"&gt;10&lt;/option&gt;
    &lt;/select&gt;
&lt;/label&gt;</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/StepPicker.razor.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components;

namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Counter.Components;

/// &lt;summary&gt;Only re-renders when Step changes, not on every increment.&lt;/summary&gt;
public partial class StepPicker
{
    [UseState(Select = nameof(SelectStep))]
    private partial int Step { get; }

    private static int SelectStep(CounterState state) =&gt; state.Step;

    private void Change(ChangeEventArgs e) =&gt;
        State&lt;CounterState&gt;().Update(s =&gt; s with { Step = int.Parse((string)e.Value!) });
}</code></pre>

<p>To select several values, return a <strong>tuple</strong>. Tuples compare by value, so the component re-renders only when one of the elements changes:</p>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/MilestoneBadge.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

@@if (Milestone.Reached)
{
    &lt;span class="badge text-bg-success"&gt;Milestone @@Milestone.Level reached&lt;/span&gt;
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/MilestoneBadge.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Counter.Components;

public partial class MilestoneBadge
{
    // Re-renders only when the milestone level changes: once every 100, not on every click.
    [UseState(Select = nameof(SelectMilestone))]
    private partial (bool Reached, int Level) Milestone { get; }

    private static (bool, int) SelectMilestone(CounterState state) =&gt; (state.Count &gt;= 100, state.Count / 100);
}</code></pre>

<p>Selector rules (checked by analyzer <code>RFEVT008</code>): the selector must be <code>static</code>, take the state type as its only parameter, and return the property's type. Tuple element names don't have to match.</p>

<h2 id="5-put-it-on-a-page">5. Put it on a page</h2>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Pages/CounterPage.razor *@@
@@page "/counter-state"
@@rendermode InteractiveAuto
@@using MyApp.Client.Features.Counter.Components

&lt;CounterDisplay /&gt;
&lt;MilestoneBadge /&gt;
&lt;div class="d-flex gap-3 align-items-end"&gt;
    &lt;CounterButtons /&gt;
    &lt;StepPicker /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

<p>Click <em>+ step</em>: <code>CounterDisplay</code> updates on every click, <code>StepPicker</code> never re-renders, and <code>MilestoneBadge</code> re-renders only when crossing a multiple of 100.</p>

<h2 id="6-read-state-outside-the-markup">6. Read state outside the markup</h2>

<p>Generated <code>[UseState]</code> properties are ready <strong>before</strong> <code>OnInitialized</code> runs, so you can use them during initialization:</p>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/CounterSince.razor.cs
namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Counter.Components;

public partial class CounterSince
{
    private int _startedAt;

    [UseState]
    private partial CounterState Counter { get; }

    // Already holds the current value: registration runs before OnInitialized.
    protected override void OnInitialized() =&gt; _startedAt = Counter.Count;
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/Components/CounterSince.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;small&gt;+@@(Counter.Count - _startedAt) since this component appeared&lt;/small&gt;</code></pre>

<p>For a one-off read where you don't want re-renders, use <code>State&lt;CounterState&gt;().Value</code>.</p>

<h2 id="7-state-that-holds-a-collection">7. State that holds a collection</h2>

<p>Use immutable collections and replace them on every change:</p>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Favorites/FavoritesState.cs
using System.Collections.Immutable;

namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Favorites;

public sealed record FavoritesState(ImmutableList&lt;string&gt; Items) : IState&lt;FavoritesState&gt;
{
    public static FavoritesState Initial =&gt; new([]);

    public bool Contains(string item) =&gt; Items.Contains(item);

    public FavoritesState Toggle(string item) =&gt;
        this with { Items = Items.Contains(item) ? Items.Remove(item) : Items.Add(item) };
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Favorites/Components/FavoriteButton.razor.cs
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components;

namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Favorites.Components;

public partial class FavoriteButton
{
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Item { get; set; } = "";

    [UseState]
    private partial FavoritesState Favorites { get; }

    private void Toggle() =&gt; State&lt;FavoritesState&gt;().Update(f =&gt; f.Toggle(Item));
}</code></pre>

<pre><code>@@* MyApp.Client/Features/Favorites/Components/FavoriteButton.razor *@@
@@inherits RfEventComponent

&lt;button class="btn btn-sm btn-link" @@onclick="Toggle"&gt;@@(Favorites.Contains(Item) ? "★" : "☆")&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>

<p>Records compare collection members <strong>by reference</strong>. A new <code>ImmutableList</code> instance always counts as a change (which is why <code>Toggle</code> returning a new list notifies), and mutating a list in place is never seen. That's one more reason to keep state immutable.</p>

<h2 id="8-read-state-from-a-service">8. Read state from a service</h2>

<p>Services use the same stores through <code>IRfEventHub</code>:</p>

<pre><code>// MyApp.Client/Features/Counter/CounterLogger.cs
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

namespace MyApp.Client.Features.Counter;

/// &lt;summary&gt;Scoped service that logs every counter change.&lt;/summary&gt;
public sealed class CounterLogger : IDisposable
{
    private readonly IDisposable _subscription;

    public CounterLogger(IRfEventHub hub, ILogger&lt;CounterLogger&gt; logger) =&gt;
        _subscription = hub.State&lt;CounterState&gt;().Subscribe(s =&gt; logger.LogInformation("Counter is now {Count}", s.Count));

    public void Dispose() =&gt; _subscription.Dispose();
}</code></pre>

<p>Store callbacks run <strong>synchronously on the writer's thread</strong>. Keep them short and don't touch UI from them. Register the service as <strong>Scoped</strong> (see <a href="/events/tutorials/07-services-and-background-work">tutorial 07</a>).</p>

<h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>

<ul>
<li>The <strong>root hub</strong> creates a store for each state type the first time it's requested, using <code>Initial</code>.</li>
<li><code>Set</code>/<code>Update</code> compare the new value to the old one. If they're equal, nothing happens. Otherwise subscribers are notified.</li>
<li>Components are notified through their own <code>InvokeAsync</code>, so writing from any thread is safe, and several quick writes collapse into one render with the latest value.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="-best-practices">Best practices</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>One state type per concept, in its feature folder</strong> (<code>Features/Counter/CounterState.cs</code>). Big "app state" records make every selector work harder and couple unrelated features.</li>
<li><strong>Name states <code>…State</code></strong> so they're easy to tell apart from events.</li>
<li><strong>Put domain logic on the record</strong> (<code>FavoritesState.Toggle</code>) instead of in components. Every writer then applies the same rules.</li>
<li><strong>Writers use <code>State&lt;T&gt;()</code>, readers use <code>[UseState]</code>.</strong> Don't add <code>[UseState]</code> to a component that only writes: it would re-render for nothing.</li>
<li><strong>Use a selector when a component reads one or two fields.</strong> It's the cheapest re-render optimization available.</li>
<li><strong>Use <code>ImmutableList</code>/<code>ImmutableDictionary</code> inside state</strong>, and replace them on change.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>

<div class="table-container"><table class="table is-striped">
<thead>
<tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Component doesn't update</td><td>It reads <code>State&lt;T&gt;().Value</code> in markup</td><td>Use <code>[UseState]</code> for anything rendered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Updates are ignored</td><td>State mutated in place (e.g. <code>list.Add</code>)</td><td>Replace the value: <code>Update(s =&gt; s with { … })</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Every write re-renders even when nothing changed</td><td>State is a <code>class</code> (<code>RFEVT013</code>)</td><td>Make it a <code>record</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>RFEVT008</code></td><td>Selector isn't static, has the wrong parameter, or returns the wrong type</td><td>Match <code>static TProperty Select(TState state)</code></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

